@meta
id: 29-16
title: Scenario — the webhook topic list
chapter: 29
type: scenario
skills: surround, commentary, fuzzy-finder, packadd, macro-record
par: 135
minutes: 14
vscode: four tabs, Ctrl-P twice, Ctrl-/ twice, and a lot of clicking between quotes
open: src/domain/webhook/webhook.topics.ts
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall! | set hidden

@brief
A ticket.

    WHK-207  The webhook topic list does not compile: the topics are
             bare identifiers rather than strings. While you are in
             there, the dispatcher's topic guard was commented out
             during last month's incident and never restored, and the
             two debug logs that replaced it are still shipping
             customer payloads to the log aggregator.

All three of this chapter's plugins are shipped with this lesson, under
`pack/dojo/start/`. You installed none of them. `ys` `cs` `ds`, `gc`
`gcc` and `:Files` are all available.

Four jobs. Only the first file is open; find the others.

  1. `src/domain/webhook/webhook.topics.ts` — every topic in the array
     needs to be a single-quoted string. The names do not change.

  2. `src/domain/webhook/webhook.dispatcher.ts` — restore the
     `isKnownTopic` guard, which is sitting there commented out.

  3. Same file — the two `console.log` lines go out of service, but
     comment them out rather than deleting them; the incident review
     still wants to see what was being logged.

  4. `docs/webhooks.md` — `webhookSecret` and `retryLimit` are prose in
     that doc and should be code, like every other identifier in it.

`src/domain/order/order.provider.ts` has nothing wrong with it. Change it
and the check fails.

@keys
| `:Files {fragment}` | open the best fuzzy match |
| `ys{motion}{char}` | add a pair |
| `gc{motion}` `gcc` | toggle comments |
| `^` | first non-blank — the array lines are indented |
| `qq` … `q` then `4@q` | five near-identical lines |
| `:w` `:wqa` | write this one, write and quit everything |

@hints
Do the topics file first, it is open already. Five lines that differ only
in the name is a macro, and the motion inside it has to start at the
first non-blank or the quotes land in the indentation.
--
For the dispatcher: the guard is three commented lines, the logs are two
uncommented ones. `gc` is a toggle, so it is the same verb both times —
only the motion differs. `gc2j` and `gcj`.
--
Fragments that pin the files down: `whdis` for the dispatcher, `docweb`
for the doc. Neither of them is ambiguous in this tree.
--
The topics: `j`, then `qq` `^` `yst,'` `j` `q`, then `4@q`. The `^` has
to be inside the macro, because `j` keeps the column.

@solution
A route that measures about 120 keystrokes.

**webhook.topics.ts** — `j` onto the first topic. `qq` `^` `yst,'` `j`
`q` records one line: go to the first non-blank, surround everything up
to the comma with single quotes, step down. `4@q` finishes the other
four. `:w`.

**webhook.dispatcher.ts** — `:Files whdis` `Enter`. `/if (!this`
`Enter` then `gc2j` uncomments the three guard lines. `/console` `Enter`
then `gcj` comments the two log lines. `:w`.

**docs/webhooks.md** — `:Files docweb` `Enter`. `/webhookSecret` `Enter`
``ysiw` ``, `/retryLimit` `Enter` ``ysiw` ``. `:wqa`.

Nothing here is a new key. Every one of them arrived in the last eleven
lessons, and the job is a normal afternoon's ticket: a compile error, a
guard to restore, some logging to silence, a doc to tidy. That is the
test — not whether you know `ys`, but whether it is the thing your hand
does when a string needs quotes.

Three habits the route is built on, all of them older than this chapter.

**Reach for a repeat before the second edit, not the sixth.** Five
quoted topics is exactly the size where recording feels like overkill
and is not. The recording cost two keystrokes, `qq` and `q`.

**`^`, not `0`, inside a macro.** `j` preserves the column, so a macro
that starts its work wherever the cursor happened to land will quietly
put the quote in the indentation on line three. This is the single most
common way a macro that worked once fails four times.

**`gc` is a toggle, so stop deciding which way round it goes.** Put the
cursor on the range and press it. Half the value of a toggle is not
having to look first.

And the thing to take out of the whole chapter: everything you just did
was done by three files of vimscript sitting next to your code, under a
hundred and fifty lines between them, doing nothing you could not have done by hand.
Plugins are not a separate category of software. When one misbehaves,
open it and read it — it is the same vim you have been learning, written
down.

@start pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim
" dojo-surround.vim - a small, honest surround plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   ds{char}           delete the surrounding pair
"   cs{old}{new}       change one surrounding pair into another
"   ys{motion}{char}   surround the text the motion covers
"   {Visual}S{char}    surround the selection
"
" Brackets come in pairs: ( [ { < all give you the matching closer.
" Anything else is used on both sides, so " gives "..." and ` gives `...`.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_surround')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_surround = 1

let s:pairs = {
      \ '(': '()', ')': '()',
      \ '[': '[]', ']': '[]',
      \ '{': '{}', '}': '{}',
      \ '<': '<>', '>': '<>',
      \ }

function! s:delims(char) abort
  return get(s:pairs, a:char, a:char . a:char)
endfunction

" The text between the pair, without the pair itself.
function! s:inner(char) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = ''
  silent execute 'normal! "zyi' . a:char
  let text = @z
  let @z = save
  return text
endfunction

" A visual selection covering the pair AND what is between it. vi" selects
" the inside; o h o l then walks each end out by one character onto the
" delimiters. Doing it this way rather than with va" matters: va" also
" swallows the whitespace next to the string.
function! s:select(char) abort
  return 'vi' . a:char . 'ohol'
endfunction

function! s:replace(char, text) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = a:text
  silent execute 'normal! ' . s:select(a:char) . '"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:ds(char) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:char)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  call s:replace(a:char, inner)
endfunction

function! s:cs(old, new) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:old)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  let d = s:delims(a:new)
  call s:replace(a:old, d[0] . inner . d[1])
endfunction

function! s:ys(type) abort
  let d = s:delims(nr2char(getchar()))
  let save = @z
  if a:type ==# 'line'
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zy"
    call setreg('z', d[0] . "\n" . @z . d[1], 'V')
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zp"
  else
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zy"
    let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zp"
  endif
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:vs(char) abort
  let d = s:delims(a:char)
  let save = @z
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zy'
  let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> ds :<C-u>call <SID>ds(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> cs :<C-u>call <SID>cs(nr2char(getchar()), nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> ys :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>ys<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> S :<C-u>call <SID>vs(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>

@start pack/dojo/start/commentary/plugin/commentary.vim
" dojo-commentary.vim - a small, honest commenting plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   gcc          toggle the comment on this line
"   gc{motion}   toggle the comment on every line the motion covers
"   {Visual}gc   toggle the comment on the selection
"
" It never guesses the comment syntax. It reads 'commentstring', which the
" filetype plugin already set: // %s for typescript, -- %s for sql,
" <!-- %s --> for markdown, # %s for yaml.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_commentary')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_commentary = 1

function! s:parts() abort
  let cms = &commentstring =~# '%s' ? &commentstring : '# %s'
  let at = stridx(cms, '%s')
  return [substitute(strpart(cms, 0, at), '\s*$', '', ''),
        \ substitute(strpart(cms, at + 2), '^\s*', '', '')]
endfunction

function! s:rx(text) abort
  return escape(a:text, '\.*$^~[]/')
endfunction

function! s:toggle(first, last) abort
  let [open, close] = s:parts()
  let head = '^\(\s*\)' . s:rx(open) . ' \?'
  let tail = ' \?' . s:rx(close) . '\s*$'
  let lines = getline(a:first, a:last)
  let code = filter(copy(lines), 'v:val =~# "\\S"')
  if empty(code)
    return
  endif
  " Uncomment only when every non-blank line is already commented.
  " Otherwise a half-commented block would flip into a mess.
  let strip = 1
  let indent = 999
  for line in code
    let strip = strip && line =~# head
    let indent = min([indent, match(line, '\S')])
  endfor
  let n = a:first
  for line in lines
    if line =~# '\S'
      if strip
        let out = substitute(line, head, '\1', '')
        let out = close ==# '' ? out : substitute(out, tail, '', '')
      else
        let body = open . ' ' . strpart(line, indent)
        let out = strpart(line, 0, indent) . (close ==# '' ? body : body . ' ' . close)
      endif
      call setline(n, out)
    endif
    let n += 1
  endfor
endfunction

function! s:opfunc(type) abort
  call s:toggle(line("'["), line("']"))
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> gcc :<C-u>call <SID>toggle(line('.'), line('.'))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> gc :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>opfunc<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> gc :<C-u>call <SID>toggle(line("'<"), line("'>"))<CR>

@start pack/dojo/start/files/plugin/files.vim
" dojo-files.vim - a fuzzy file finder in thirty lines.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   :Files {pattern}   open the file whose path best matches {pattern}
"   :Files <Tab>       cycle the candidates on the command line
"
" The ranking is matchfuzzy(), which vim has had built in since 8.2. The
" letters you type have to appear in the path in order, and nothing else.
" `opr` finds order.provider.ts. That is the whole trick fzf sells.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_files')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_files = 1

function! s:index() abort
  let all = split(globpath('.', '**/*'), "\n")
  call filter(all, 'filereadable(v:val)')
  call map(all, 'substitute(v:val, "^\\./", "", "")')
  " A real finder skips .git, node_modules and build output. This one skips
  " the package directory, so the list is the project and nothing else.
  return filter(all, 'v:val !~# "^pack/"')
endfunction

function! s:matches(pat) abort
  let all = s:index()
  return a:pat ==# '' ? all : matchfuzzy(all, a:pat)
endfunction

function! s:complete(lead, line, pos) abort
  return s:matches(a:lead)
endfunction

function! s:files(pat) abort
  let hits = s:matches(a:pat)
  if empty(hits)
    echohl ErrorMsg
    echo 'Files: nothing matches ' . a:pat
    echohl NONE
    return
  endif
  execute 'edit ' . fnameescape(hits[0])
endfunction

command! -nargs=? -complete=customlist,s:complete Files call s:files(<q-args>)

